1
00:00:02,195 --> 00:00:04,999
So what's the time complexity of the stack

2
00:00:04,999 --> 00:00:07,627
data structure and how does it compare to

3
00:00:07,643 --> 00:00:08,011
arrays?

4
00:00:08,588 --> 00:00:12,066
I'm comparing it to arrays because stacks,

5
00:00:12,114 --> 00:00:14,341
like arrays, are list structures.

6
00:00:14,998 --> 00:00:16,504
So let's compare stacks to arrays.

7
00:00:17,177 --> 00:00:19,421
What about accessing an element?

8
00:00:19,965 --> 00:00:22,353
Well, in a stack we have only one way of

9
00:00:22,385 --> 00:00:24,676
getting access to an element and that is

10
00:00:25,029 --> 00:00:28,139
that we pop the element off. Right? If we do

11
00:00:28,155 --> 00:00:31,011
that, that's of course O because it's always

12
00:00:31,043 --> 00:00:33,032
the last element. That's very easy to

13
00:00:33,032 --> 00:00:35,054
target. It doesn't need to go through the

14
00:00:35,054 --> 00:00:36,064
entire stack for that.

15
00:00:36,642 --> 00:00:38,343
But of course, we're also limited to that.

16
00:00:38,744 --> 00:00:40,091
And just to make this very clear, I'm

17
00:00:40,107 --> 00:00:43,043
talking about pop here. And pop, no matter

18
00:00:43,316 --> 00:00:45,305
if we implement the stack with the linked

19
00:00:45,305 --> 00:00:46,845
list or with the array,

20
00:00:47,407 --> 00:00:50,904
pop always is an O operation. The built-in

21
00:00:51,017 --> 00:00:54,082
pop method on the built-in array also is O.

22
00:00:54,227 --> 00:00:56,556
It's constant time because it's the last

23
00:00:56,620 --> 00:00:59,447
item, and for that JavaScript doesn't need

24
00:00:59,447 --> 00:01:01,792
to go through the entire array. That's why

25
00:01:01,792 --> 00:01:02,547
it's O.

26
00:01:03,463 --> 00:01:05,888
But again, as mentioned, of course limited

27
00:01:06,049 --> 00:01:08,603
to the top element. We can't get access to

28
00:01:08,603 --> 00:01:09,262
another element.

29
00:01:09,984 --> 00:01:13,261
For arrays, it's also O, and we can access

30
00:01:13,406 --> 00:01:15,735
any element because we can access them by

31
00:01:15,815 --> 00:01:17,887
index. So if we don't need to search an

32
00:01:17,984 --> 00:01:20,972
element but we just want to get access to it

33
00:01:20,972 --> 00:01:23,173
and we know the position, it's O.

34
00:01:23,767 --> 00:01:25,920
And then arrays are more flexible than

35
00:01:25,952 --> 00:01:26,418
stacks.

36
00:01:27,237 --> 00:01:28,892
Now what about insertion at the end?

37
00:01:29,711 --> 00:01:33,021
Well, if we define the end as the top of the

38
00:01:33,021 --> 00:01:36,025
stack, it's O obviously because we just push

39
00:01:36,234 --> 00:01:38,884
a new element. That's always O and that's

40
00:01:38,884 --> 00:01:41,840
therefore also O if we're talking about

41
00:01:41,840 --> 00:01:42,708
regular arrays.

42
00:01:43,784 --> 00:01:45,342
What about insertion at the beginning?

43
00:01:46,258 --> 00:01:49,568
Well, for a stack, that's technically not

44
00:01:49,568 --> 00:01:53,810
possible if we define the end as the topmost

45
00:01:53,810 --> 00:01:56,445
element. If we define the first element as

46
00:01:56,477 --> 00:01:58,324
the topmost element, it is possible, but

47
00:01:58,469 --> 00:01:59,931
then insertion at the end wouldn't be

48
00:01:59,931 --> 00:02:00,429
possible.

49
00:02:00,959 --> 00:02:03,514
But if we think of insertion at the end as

50
00:02:03,707 --> 00:02:05,956
pushing and as the topmost element,

51
00:02:06,358 --> 00:02:08,463
insertion at the beginning is not possible,

52
00:02:08,672 --> 00:02:11,917
or it is possible but only by popping off

53
00:02:11,917 --> 00:02:14,917
all all our elements and that's why I wrote

54
00:02:15,094 --> 00:02:16,746
O with data loss here.

55
00:02:17,356 --> 00:02:20,260
We can pop off all elements and for that we

56
00:02:20,260 --> 00:02:23,420
need to go through all elements, hence O,

57
00:02:23,934 --> 00:02:27,142
but of course those elements are lost or we

58
00:02:27,142 --> 00:02:28,923
need to store them somewhere else and then

59
00:02:28,923 --> 00:02:31,314
it becomes super inefficient at some point.

60
00:02:32,100 --> 00:02:34,683
For regular arrays, we don't have that data

61
00:02:34,683 --> 00:02:35,405
loss part

62
00:02:35,950 --> 00:02:38,790
but we also have O. So if you wanna insert

63
00:02:38,790 --> 00:02:41,358
something at the beginning, neither stacks

64
00:02:41,358 --> 00:02:44,279
nor arrays are the best possible choice as

65
00:02:44,311 --> 00:02:44,760
it seems.

66
00:02:45,579 --> 00:02:47,890
Keep in mind that here the linked list was

67
00:02:48,002 --> 00:02:50,682
pretty good because there we had O for

68
00:02:50,714 --> 00:02:52,592
insertion at the beginning, but that's just

69
00:02:52,656 --> 00:02:54,967
a side note. So if you need to do that, the

70
00:02:54,967 --> 00:02:56,235
linked list might be worth a look.

71
00:02:57,149 --> 00:02:59,027
What about insertion in the middle?

72
00:02:59,637 --> 00:03:01,884
Well, it's the same as with insertion at the

73
00:03:01,884 --> 00:03:02,333
beginning.

74
00:03:02,927 --> 00:03:05,542
Technically it's not possible, Or it's only

75
00:03:05,558 --> 00:03:08,829
possible if we remove all items on top of

76
00:03:08,877 --> 00:03:11,507
the item we wanna insert something on. So we

77
00:03:11,507 --> 00:03:14,409
have O, we need to remove all the items up

78
00:03:14,473 --> 00:03:16,237
to the place where we wanna insert

79
00:03:16,237 --> 00:03:16,573
something,

80
00:03:17,183 --> 00:03:18,898
and the data we remove is lost.

81
00:03:19,444 --> 00:03:22,009
For arrays, insertion in the middle is also

82
00:03:22,089 --> 00:03:22,362
O.

83
00:03:23,613 --> 00:03:25,649
And now what about searching elements? If

84
00:03:25,681 --> 00:03:27,942
you wanna search an element in a stack,

85
00:03:28,022 --> 00:03:30,684
again, since we can only access the topmost

86
00:03:30,732 --> 00:03:33,861
item, it's not possible. Or only possible

87
00:03:34,358 --> 00:03:37,168
with data loss. So only if we,

88
00:03:37,714 --> 00:03:40,123
well, again remove all the items up to the

89
00:03:40,203 --> 00:03:41,777
item we were looking for,

90
00:03:42,547 --> 00:03:45,710
then it's O. And for arrays, it's also O.

91
00:03:46,577 --> 00:03:49,066
So as we can tell, the stack data structure

92
00:03:49,130 --> 00:03:52,567
has no area where it does better than the

93
00:03:52,567 --> 00:03:53,546
regular array.

94
00:03:54,108 --> 00:03:56,693
And therefore, the reason for using a stack

95
00:03:56,822 --> 00:03:57,592
over an array

96
00:03:58,266 --> 00:04:00,240
wouldn't be that you have a better

97
00:04:00,288 --> 00:04:02,182
performance or anything like that.

98
00:04:02,760 --> 00:04:05,616
Instead, the reasons for using a stack would

99
00:04:05,728 --> 00:04:08,472
be that you want to simplify an array, that

100
00:04:08,472 --> 00:04:10,606
you want to restrict the options of

101
00:04:10,638 --> 00:04:12,885
interacting with that array, that you want

102
00:04:12,885 --> 00:04:15,244
to restrict it to only pushing and popping

103
00:04:15,613 --> 00:04:18,228
because that might be what you need to do in

104
00:04:18,325 --> 00:04:20,298
a specific part of your application.

105
00:04:20,908 --> 00:04:22,866
And you want to ensure that no one is able

106
00:04:22,914 --> 00:04:24,759
to accidentally add something in the

107
00:04:24,759 --> 00:04:26,780
beginning, in in the middle, remove

108
00:04:26,780 --> 00:04:28,897
something from there, or anything like that.

109
00:04:29,410 --> 00:04:32,071
So it's not a performance optimization, it's

110
00:04:32,071 --> 00:04:32,857
more a

111
00:04:33,418 --> 00:04:36,545
use case optimization where sometimes we

112
00:04:36,545 --> 00:04:39,511
might want less options. And I did mention

113
00:04:39,511 --> 00:04:42,429
some examples earlier where such stack data

114
00:04:42,461 --> 00:04:42,975
structures

115
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:46,213
do occur in real life, and therefore, of

116
00:04:46,246 --> 00:04:48,538
course, we do have use cases for stacks.
